% ============================================================================
% contraction — a contraction and the two-word phrase it stands for
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A contraction combines two words into a shorter one, marking the missing
% letters with an apostrophe: "do not" becomes "don't." Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? contraction(dont, $Expansion)
%       % do_not
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `contraction(word, expansion)` carrying the source citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the expansion
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% contraction the source does not expand this way. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind an expansion and recall which contraction it names:
%
%     ? contraction($Word, will_not)
%       % wont
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `word` and `expansion` are apostrophe-free atom labels
% rather than the punctuated spellings: ADJ atom labels cannot contain an
% apostrophe, so this table uses the same short atom-style-label discipline
% `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj` already established for
% punctuation-bearing content — `dont` stands for "don't," and the
% underscore-joined `do_not` stands for the two-word expansion. The `source`
% citation string below still quotes the ORIGINAL punctuated text, so the
% mapping remains independently checkable against the cited page.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "What Are Contractions in
% Writing? Definition and Examples" article, whose contraction-list tables
% give each contraction's expansion directly:
%
%     word (this table's atom label)   expansion (atom label)   source pair (verbatim)
%     --------------------------------  ------------------------  ---------------------------------
%     dont                               do_not                    "don't" = "do not"
%     cant                               can_not                   "can't" = "can not"
%     wont                               will_not                  "won't" = "will not"
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same
% source family already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`.
% Note the source's own table expands "can't" to the TWO-word "can not,"
% not the more common single-word spelling "cannot" — this table uses
% exactly what the source states, not a silently "corrected" spelling.
% A contraction that is NOT one of these three — e.g. "shouldn't," a real
% contraction, but not one this specific cited page's table this row set
% draws from — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing an expansion. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table contraction {
    columns word, expansion

    row (dont, do_not)
    row (cant, can_not)
    row (wont, will_not)

    source "don't = do not"
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/grammar/contractions/"
    trust consensus
}
